{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-15647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}$ and the point $P(21,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-15647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(21,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt[3]{3x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2/3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(21) & =\\frac{1}{(64)^{2/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(21,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,21)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(21)} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-15647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ece475a9bde3bf8a65c0cfc23fcb4d96e7fe7072363ad6a0a03d3cb598e8baa9"} {"question" "q-15647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-15647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-15647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-7$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(1)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ece475a9bde3bf8a65c0cfc23fcb4d96e7fe7072363ad6a0a03d3cb598e8baa9"} {"question" "q-342588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $-8$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ece475a9bde3bf8a65c0cfc23fcb4d96e7fe7072363ad6a0a03d3cb598e8baa9"} {"question" "q-342592" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342592" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={5}^{x}+3.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ {5}^{x}+3 & =4 \\\\ {5}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {5}^{x} & ={5}^{0} \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={5}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ece475a9bde3bf8a65c0cfc23fcb4d96e7fe7072363ad6a0a03d3cb598e8baa9"} {"question" "q-342624" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342624" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\ln(5)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+\\frac{x+3}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+\\frac{2+3}{2-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ece475a9bde3bf8a65c0cfc23fcb4d96e7fe7072363ad6a0a03d3cb598e8baa9"} {"question" "q-342625" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"} {"question" "q-342625" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005848/state.json" "file_sha256" "253562712d0494138bf79ce0be4310020dd0ba18b56e89fd192d5f2650e40e35"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1109 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
